<Article
  title="How Independent Verification Works"
  heading="How it works"
  kicker="Cornerstone"
  description="What independent verification on a residential project actually involves, in sequence: contract, bid comparison, payment applications, change orders, holdback, lien period, release."
>
  <p>
    “Owner’s representation” sounds abstract until you see the sequence. This page walks the whole of it —
    what independent verification actually involves, from the contract to the final release — so that by the
    end, the service is not a concept but a checklist you could hold us to. First, though, the reason the
    sequence exists at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Chapter one: who works for whom</h2>
  <p>
    There are no villains in this account. The general contractor’s project manager is competent, probably
    honest, and employed by the contractor: when scope is ambiguous, their obligation runs to the company
    paying their salary. The architect’s deepest loyalty is to the design. The engineers protect their
    stamps, as they should. The lender’s inspector protects the loan collateral. Your lawyer reads the
    contract once, before signing, and returns to their practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each of these people is doing their job well. The difficulty is that nobody at the table has, as their
    job, your whole position — cost, schedule, quality and risk, held together, on your side only. Some
    builders narrow the visibility gap with open books, and that is worth having. But visibility into a
    counterparty’s records is not the same as independent confirmation of the work behind them — and every
    step below exists because of that difference.
  </p>

  <Plate
    src="/plates/plate-05-linework.svg"
