  <h2>Step one: the contract, before it is signed</h2>
  <p>
    Everything downstream is played from the position the paperwork creates, so verification starts before
    there is anything to verify. The agreement is read the way its drafter reads it: payment mechanics,
    change-order pricing, delay and termination provisions, warranty terms, and — decisively — whether the
    milestones that trigger payments are defined tightly enough that a third party can later check them.
    A schedule of values structured for verification is negotiated here, once, when it costs nothing.
    Your lawyer receives a negotiation agenda, not a pile of paper.
  </p>

  <h2>Step two: the bid comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Two bids that look alike can carry entirely different risk. Line items are benchmarked against an
    independent cost opinion; allowances are tested for realism; exclusions are surfaced and priced;
    front-loading — generous values on early work, lean values on late — is caught while it is still a
    negotiating point rather than a sunk cost. The original bid is the last number on the project that
    competition ever disciplines. It deserves the attention.
  </p>

  <h2>Step three: payment application review</h2>
  <p>
    Once construction begins, a document arrives monthly asking for money. It asserts percentages complete,
    materials stored, milestones reached — and it is prepared by the party being paid. Each application is
    read against the schedule of values and prior draws, and the claimed work is verified in place: is the
    framing billed at eighty percent at eighty percent? Are the stored materials on site, insured, and yours
    if things go wrong? The recommendation — release, hold, or release in part — is written down, with
    reasons, before the payment deadline.
  </p>

  <h2>Step four: change order interrogation</h2>
  <p>
    Every change is asked the same three questions. Was it in scope — or is contract-price work returning
    with a markup? Is the price supported — documented cost under the contract’s fixed markup, or “to be
    agreed”? What does it do to the schedule — and does “no schedule impact” survive contact with the
    sequence? A running ledger tracks the aggregate against contingency, because no single change order
    sinks a project; the accumulation does.
  </p>

  <h2>Step five: the holdback</h2>
  <p>
    In British Columbia, s.4(1) of the Builders Lien Act requires the owner, as the person primarily liable
    on the head contract, to retain a 10% holdback — a duty the statute imposes rather than a term anyone
    negotiates, and one the builder cannot discharge for you, because the holdback exists to protect the
    owner <em>from</em> the contracting chain. The retained amount is calculated on every draw, paid into
    the jointly administered account where s.5 requires one, and held to the statutory calendar.
    <a href="/services/holdback-and-draw-compliance">The full mechanics are here.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Step six: the lien period</h2>
  <p>
    After the statutory trigger, parties down the chain have 45 days to file a claim of lien (s.20), and the
    holdback period itself runs 55 days (s.8) — two clocks that are constantly confused and govern different
    things. The dates are calendared from the trigger events, not from memory; the closeout paperwork —
    deficiency lists, warranties, as-builts — runs in parallel, while the last leverage a project has still
    exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Step seven: the release</h2>
  <p>
    Before holdback money moves: a title search, confirming what has actually been filed against the
    property; a reconciliation of the final account; and a written release approval with the evidence
    attached. The project ends the way it ran — with a document, not a handshake — and the file, complete
    from step one, is yours to keep. If a dispute ever comes, that file is your position.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the sequence starts so early</h2>
  <p>
    Owner attention and owner leverage run in opposite directions. Leverage is highest before signatures —
    when terms can still be negotiated and walking away costs nothing but time. Attention peaks
    mid-construction, when a number arrives that doesn’t make sense — and by then the contract is fixed, the
    buyout is done, and the early draws are paid.
  </p>

  <RiskLadder />

  <p>
    That is why the entry engagement is a <a href="/services/pre-construction-audit">pre-construction
